External knowledge that might be helpful: 
Categories: Geospatial functions


## ST_WITHIN

Returns true if the first geospatial object is fully contained by the second geospatial object. In other words:

The first GEOGRAPHY object g1 is fully contained by the second GEOGRAPHY object g2.
The first GEOMETRY object g1 is fully contained by the second GEOMETRY object g2.

Calling ST_WITHIN(g1, g2) is equivalent to calling ST_CONTAINS(g2, g1).
Although ST_COVEREDBY and ST_WITHIN might seem similar, the two functions have subtle differences. For details on the differences between “covered by” and “within”, see the Dimensionally Extended 9-Intersection Model (DE-9IM).

Note This function does not support using a GeometryCollection or FeatureCollection as input values.

Tip You can use the search optimization service to improve the performance of queries that call this function.
For details, see Search Optimization Service.

See also:ST_CONTAINS , ST_COVEREDBY


## Syntax

ST_WITHIN( <geography_expression_1> , <geography_expression_2> )

ST_WITHIN( <geometry_expression_1> , <geometry_expression_2> )


## Arguments


geography_expression_1A GEOGRAPHY object that is not a GeometryCollection or FeatureCollection.

geography_expression_2A GEOGRAPHY object that is not a GeometryCollection or FeatureCollection.

geometry_expression_1A GEOMETRY object that is not a GeometryCollection or FeatureCollection.

geometry_expression_2A GEOMETRY object that is not a GeometryCollection or FeatureCollection.


## Returns

BOOLEAN.

## Examples


## GEOGRAPHY examples

This shows a simple use of the ST_WITHIN function:

create table geospatial_table_01 (g1 GEOGRAPHY, g2 GEOGRAPHY);
insert into geospatial_table_01 (g1, g2) values 
    ('POLYGON((0 0, 3 0, 3 3, 0 3, 0 0))', 'POLYGON((1 1, 2 1, 2 2, 1 2, 1 1))');

Copy SELECT ST_WITHIN(g1, g2) 
    FROM geospatial_table_01;
+-------------------+
| ST_WITHIN(G1, G2) |
|-------------------|
| False             |
+-------------------+
The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'NOAA_DATA_PLUS': {'GEO_US_BOUNDARIES': ['ZIP_CODES'], 'NOAA_GSOD': [], 'NOAA_HISTORIC_SEVERE_STORMS': [], 'NOAA_HURRICANES': ['HURRICANES'], 'NOAA_ICOADS': [], 'NOAA_PASSIVE_ACOUSTIC_INDEX': [], 'NOAA_PASSIVE_BIOACOUSTIC': [], 'NOAA_PIFSC_METADATA': [], 'NOAA_PRELIMINARY_SEVERE_STORMS': [], 'NOAA_SIGNIFICANT_EARTHQUAKES': [], 'NOAA_TSUNAMI': []}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage
• NOAA_DATA_PLUS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.ZIP_CODES  
  – "city": city to be returned in the result set.  
  – "zip_code": 5‑digit postal code to be returned in the result set.  
  – "state_code": two‑letter state abbreviation to be returned in the result set.  
  – "zip_code_geom": polygon geometry that defines the spatial boundary of the ZIP code; needed for the spatial comparison.  

• NOAA_DATA_PLUS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES  
  – "name": storm name; filter out blanks / ‘UNNAMED’.  
  – "latitude": storm center latitude.  
  – "longitude": storm center longitude.  
  – "sid": unique storm identifier; useful when de‑duplicating multiple 6‑hourly fixes from the same storm.  
  – "iso_time": timestamp of the fix; may be used later to pick representative points or for extra filters.  

Key condition that will be required later
ST_WITHIN( ST_MAKEPOINT("HUR"."longitude", "HUR"."latitude") ,
           "ZIP"."zip_code_geom" )

Below are 10 step‑by‑step Snowflake SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that progressively explore the data and show how one would arrive at the final answer.  
All column names are enclosed in double quotes, and each query is preceded by a short description.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Preview basic ZIP‑code information (first 20 rows)
SELECT 
    "city",
    "zip_code",
    "state_code",
    "area_land_meters"
FROM NOAA_DATA_PLUS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.ZIP_CODES
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: List distinct hurricane names (excluding blanks) for a quick look
SELECT DISTINCT 
    "name"
FROM NOAA_DATA_PLUS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES
WHERE "name" IS NOT NULL
  AND TRIM("name") <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Show sample hurricane fixes with lat/lon and name
SELECT 
    "sid",
    "name",
    "latitude",
    "longitude",
    "iso_time"
FROM NOAA_DATA_PLUS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES
WHERE "latitude" IS NOT NULL
  AND "longitude" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Verify that a point geometry can be built from lat/lon
SELECT 
    "sid",
    ST_ASWKT( ST_MAKEPOINT("longitude", "latitude") ) AS "wkt_point"
FROM NOAA_DATA_PLUS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES
WHERE "latitude" IS NOT NULL
  AND "longitude" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Count how many hurricane fixes fall inside an arbitrary ZIP (e.g., 33109, Miami Beach)
SELECT 
    COUNT(*) AS "fixes_inside_zip"
FROM NOAA_DATA_PLUS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES HUR
JOIN NOAA_DATA_PLUS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.ZIP_CODES ZIP
      ON ZIP."zip_code" = '33109'
WHERE ST_WITHIN( ST_MAKEPOINT(HUR."longitude", HUR."latitude"), ZIP."zip_code_geom" )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Retrieve distinct storm IDs that ever entered ZIP 33109 (still limited)
SELECT DISTINCT 
    HUR."sid",
    HUR."name"
